  <p>
    Before you pour more budget into
    <a href="/services/local-seo">local SEO</a> or
    <a href="/services/paid-ads">Google ads</a>, make sure Google actually knows what you do. This is
    the category mistake that quietly killed leads for an electrician we later helped.
  </p>

  <h2>The setup</h2>
  <p>
    Early in building Local Service Rocket, I took on an electrician who was already paying an SEO
    company. They weren’t getting leads. I turned on Google Ads using processes that were working for
    another client, and still nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the same ad system works in one account and dies in another, you stop blaming “the
    algorithm” and start inspecting the foundation.
  </p>

  <h2>What was wrong</h2>
  <p>
    On their Google Business Profile, the <strong>primary category</strong> wasn’t “Electrician.”
    It was something like “Electrical installation service.”
  </p>
  <p>
    That sounds close. It isn’t. I checked Google Keyword Planner for what people in their market
    actually type. The dominant term, by a mile, was <strong>electrician</strong>. Almost nobody
    searches for an “electrical installation service.”
  </p>
  <p>
    Google’s job is to match searchers with businesses that are clearly relevant. If your profile
    tells Google you’re a niche installation service, Google will hesitate to show you for
    “electrician near me”, and your ads inherit that confusion as poor Quality / poor fit.
  </p>

  <h2>The only change</h2>
  <p>
    We took over the Google Business Profile and changed the primary category to
    <strong>Electrician</strong> (aligned to real search demand).
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Within about a week they were getting one to three calls a day from the Google Business Profile
    alone. The ads weren’t even the hero of that turnaround.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>The principle: relevance before authority</h2>
  <p>
    SEO people love talking about blogs, backlinks, and “content strategies.” Those can matter
    later. When Google doesn’t know what you are, none of it lands.
  </p>
  <p>For a new or misunderstood brand, the order is:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Relevance:</strong> teach Google the exact services and terms you want (Keyword
      Planner truth, correct categories, clear site titles/H1s/H2s).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Authority and trust:</strong> reviews (especially recent ones), proof, awards,
      consistent NAP, strong service pages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Expansion:</strong> more topics, more service-area pages, more advanced content once
      the core is solid.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Huge brands can write poetic headlines with zero keyword in sight. Apple doesn’t need to say
    “phone” in every H1. Your plumbing company is not Apple. Until Google is 100% sure what you do,
    stay radically clear.
  </p>

  <h2>What to check this week</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Open Keyword Planner. What do homeowners type for your trade in your city?</li>
    <li>Does your GBP primary category match that language?</li>
    <li>Do your homepage title and H1 say the same thing without stuffing every paragraph?</li>
    <li>Are you winning on <em>review velocity</em> (new reviews per week), not just total stars?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Fix the boring foundation. That’s where most of the money is hiding, and where most SEO vendors
    never look hard enough.
  </p>
